   (b) review and confirm the plan or gas blend with a certified dive
       professional, technical diving instructor, or certified gas blender.

2. INHERENT RISKS OF SCUBA DIVING AND HIGH-PRESSURE GAS BLENDING
   Open-circuit (OC), closed-circuit rebreather (CCR), decompression, altitude
   diving, and high-pressure oxygen/trimix gas blending are inherently hazardous
   activities that carry severe risks, including decompression sickness (DCS),
   arterial gas embolism (AGE), pulmonary barotrauma, central nervous system
   (CNS) or pulmonary oxygen toxicity, hypoxia, hypercapnia, inert gas narcosis,
   CCR loop/sensor/scrubber failure, oxygen-enriched fire or explosion during
   blending, serious personal injury, permanent disability, or death.

3. THEORETICAL MODEL LIMITATIONS AND NO PHYSIOLOGICAL GUARANTEE
   Mathematical decompression and oxygen toxicity models are simplified
   approximations and cannot predict individual physiological susceptibility.
   Decompression sickness, oxygen toxicity, or other diving injuries can occur
   even when a diver strictly follows a computed schedule within published
   model limits.

4. CERTIFICATION, MEDICAL FITNESS, PHYSICAL GAS ANALYSIS, AND AI VERIFICATION
   Only divers and gas blenders holding appropriate agency certification,
   current medical fitness for diving (such as an RSTC Diver Medical clearance),
   and redundant dive equipment may conduct technical dives or blend breathing
   gases. Every breathing gas cylinder MUST be personally and physically
   analyzed with a calibrated oxygen (and helium, where applicable) analyzer
   and clearly labeled with its Maximum Operating Depth (MOD) before diving.
   When invoked through an AI agent or automated workflow, users must verify
   exact parameters and numerical outputs from scripts/zhl16c_planner.py
   directly to prevent transcription or interpretation errors.

